Re-initialise atfork-locked mutexes in the child on QNX.

tevent, pthreadpool and tfork lock their mutexes in a pthread_atfork()
prepare handler and unlock them in the parent and child handlers. On QNX
the child's thread does not own a mutex its parent locked:
pthread_mutex_unlock() returns EPERM (hazard 127), and these handlers
abort()/assert on that -- every fork of a threaded Samba process (smbd
forks one child per client) would die. In the child, re-initialise the
mutex instead, which leaves it unlocked as intended (the QNX ports' Samba
4.11 patch did the same).

Upstream-Status: QNX-specific

--- a/lib/pthreadpool/pthreadpool.c
+++ b/lib/pthreadpool/pthreadpool.c
@@ -329,6 +329,17 @@
 	assert(ret == 0);
 }
 
+/*
+ * A mutex locked in the pthread_atfork() prepare handler is not owned by
+ * the child's thread on QNX: pthread_mutex_unlock() fails there with EPERM.
+ * The child takes a fresh, unlocked mutex instead.
+ */
+#ifdef __QNX__
+#define pthreadpool_child_mutex_unlock(m) pthread_mutex_init((m), NULL)
+#else
+#define pthreadpool_child_mutex_unlock(m) pthread_mutex_unlock(m)
+#endif
+
 static void pthreadpool_child(void)
 {
 	int ret;
@@ -347,14 +358,14 @@
 		ret = pthread_cond_init(&pool->condvar, NULL);
 		assert(ret == 0);
 
-		ret = pthread_mutex_unlock(&pool->mutex);
+		ret = pthreadpool_child_mutex_unlock(&pool->mutex);
 		assert(ret == 0);
 
-		ret = pthread_mutex_unlock(&pool->fork_mutex);
+		ret = pthreadpool_child_mutex_unlock(&pool->fork_mutex);
 		assert(ret == 0);
 	}
 
-	ret = pthread_mutex_unlock(&pthreadpools_mutex);
+	ret = pthreadpool_child_mutex_unlock(&pthreadpools_mutex);
 	assert(ret == 0);
 }
 
